    10	existing vertex-gap functional \(R_{k,d_2}\), not an independent obstruction.
    11	The avenue does not repair the universal book-landing gap G2.
    12	
    13	All assertions below marked **EXACT** are proved algebraically in the text and
    14	checked by the SymPy 1.14.0 transcript in Section 7. Statements not proved are
    15	explicitly labelled **CONJECTURE**. No global three-sheet Keller map is claimed:
    16	Section 4 uses the unique \(\Lambda=3\) *local pole datum* as a negative control,
    17	precisely because Orevkov excludes its global realization.
    18	
    19	---
    20	
    21	## 1. The exact object
    22	
    23	Work over a characteristic-zero field \(K\). Let
    24	
    25	\[
    26	 F=(P,Q):\mathbb A^2_{x,y}\longrightarrow\mathbb A^2,
    27	 \qquad [P,Q]=P_xQ_y-P_yQ_x=c\in K^*.
    28	\]
    29	
    30	With \(\omega=dx\wedge dy=d(x\,dy)=-d(y\,dx)\), dimension two gives
    31	
    32	\[
    33	 F^*\omega=dP\wedge dQ=c\,dx\wedge dy.
    34	\]
    35	
    36	Thus \(F\) is conformally symplectic (symplectic after scaling one target
    37	coordinate). Choose the Liouville primitive \(\lambda=x\,dy\). The two polynomial
    38	**action forms** are
    39	
    40	\[
    41	 \alpha_P:=P\,dQ-cx\,dy,
    42	 \qquad
    43	 \alpha_Q:=Q\,dP-cy\,dx.                                      \tag{1.1}
    44	\]
    45	
    46	They are closed:
    47	
    48	\[
    49	 d\alpha_P=dP\wedge dQ-c\,dx\wedge dy=0,
    50	 \qquad
    51	 d\alpha_Q=dQ\wedge dP-c\,dy\wedge dx=0.                    \tag{1.2}
    52	\]
    53	
    54	Polynomial Poincare exactness on \(\mathbb A^2\) gives **polynomial** action
    55	primitives \(S_P,S_Q\in K[x,y]\), unique up to constants:
    56	
    57	\[
    58	 \alpha_P=dS_P,\qquad \alpha_Q=dS_Q.                         \tag{1.3}
    59	\]
    60	
    61	No analytic or invertibility hypothesis enters. One may prove (1.3) directly:
    62	integrate the \(dx\)-coefficient of a closed polynomial one-form term by term;
    63	closedness makes the difference a polynomial in \(y\) alone, which can also be
    64	integrated in characteristic zero. The two choices obey
    65	
    66	\[
    67	 S_P+S_Q=PQ-cxy+\text{constant},                             \tag{1.4}
    68	\]
    69	
    70	because \(\alpha_P+\alpha_Q=d(PQ-cxy)\). Equivalently, the symmetric action
    71	form
    72	
    73	\[
    74	 \tfrac12(P\,dQ-Q\,dP)-\tfrac c2(x\,dy-y\,dx)
    75	   =\tfrac12d(S_P-S_Q)
    76	\]
    77	
    78	is exact. Changing the Liouville primitive changes the action only by an exact
    79	polynomial differential, so none of the residue conclusions below changes.
    80	
    81	### 1.1 Why this is genuinely dimension-two-specific
    82	
    83	For a map of a two-dimensional affine space, the symplectic form is already a
    84	top form, so the determinant identity is exactly the symplectic identity. In
    85	dimension \(n\ge3\), \(\det JF=c\) controls an \(n\)-form; it does not imply
    86	preservation of a two-form and therefore does not produce (1.1). This is real
    87	two-dimensional structure. The experiment below shows that it is nevertheless
    88	too weak: every putative plane counterexample has it automatically.
    89	
    90	### 1.2 Poles in the standard compactification
    91	
    92	Embed \(\mathbb A^2\subset\mathbb P^2\), with \(L_\infty=\{Z=0\}\). If
    93	\(\deg S_P=d\), then on the \(X\ne0\) chart
    94	
    95	\[
    96	 u=Z/X=1/x,\qquad v=Y/X=y/x,
    97	\]
    98	
    99	one has
   100	
   101	\[
   102	 S_P=u^{-d}\bigl(S_{P,d}(1,v)+uS_{P,d-1}(1,v)+\cdots\bigr). \tag{1.5}
   103	\]
   104	
   105	At a generic point of \(L_\infty\), \(S_P\) has pole order \(d\), the normal
   106	component of \(dS_P\) has pole order \(d+1\), and the tangential component has
   107	order at most \(d\). At a zero of the leading homogeneous form these orders can
   108	drop. After any resolution \(\pi:X\to\mathbb P^2\), the pole order along a
   109	boundary component \(E\) is simply the divisorial valuation of the rational
   110	function \(\pi^*S_P\). There is no a priori forbidden order: it is the pole
   111	divisor of a polynomial.
   112	
   113	The canonical divisorial residue is fatal to the proposed obstruction. In a
   114	local parameter \(t\) at \(E\), write
   115	
   116	\[
   117	 \pi^*S_P=\sum_{m\ge-N}a_m t^m.
   118	\]
   119	
   120	The \(dt/t\)-coefficient of \(d(\pi^*S_P)\) is zero, since it could only come
   121	from \(m=0\), whose derivative coefficient is \(m a_m=0\). Hence:
   122	
   123	> **EXACT LEMMA 1 (global action residues vanish).** For every prime boundary
   124	> divisor \(E\) on every resolution,
   125	> \[
   126	> \operatorname{Res}_E(\pi^*\alpha_P)
   127	> =\operatorname{Res}_E(\pi^*\alpha_Q)=0.                   \tag{1.6}
   128	> \]
   129	> Every action period also vanishes, because the action is the differential of
   130	> a single-valued rational function.
   131	
   132	A higher polar coefficient of \(dS_P\) is not a residue. It changes with the
   133	choice of local parameter/trivialization. Pole order is intrinsic, but there is
   134	no general vanishing law for it.
   135	
   136	### 1.3 The nontrivial placewise functional
   137	
   138	There is a different, useful residue. Let \(\widetilde C_a\) be the normalization
   139	of the compactified fibre \(P=a\), and \(p\in\widetilde C_a\setminus C_a\) an
   140	infinity place. Pulling back \(\alpha_Q=dS_Q\) to \(P=a\) kills \(dP\):
   141	
   142	\[
   143	 -c\,y\,dx=d(S_Q|_{\widetilde C_a}).
   144	\]
   145	
   146	Likewise, on a fibre \(Q=b\), \(-c\,x\,dy=d(S_P|_{\widetilde C_b})\). Therefore
   147	
   148	\[
   149	 \mathcal R^P_p:=\operatorname{Res}_p(y\,dx)=0,
   150	 \qquad
   151	 \mathcal R^Q_p:=\operatorname{Res}_p(x\,dy)=0.             \tag{1.7}
   152	\]
   153	
   154	These are separate equations at every place, stronger than merely saying that
   155	their sum is zero. In the normalized Puiseux chart
   156	
   157	\[
   158	 x=t^{-\kappa},\qquad y=\sum_m c_m t^m,
   159	\]
   160	
   161	they become the coefficient selector
   162	
   163	\[
   164	 \operatorname{Res}_t(y\,dx)=-\kappa c_\kappa,
   165	 \qquad
   166	 \operatorname{Res}_t(x\,dy)=+\kappa c_\kappa.             \tag{1.8}
   167	\]
   168	
   169	Thus exactness forces \(c_\kappa=0\). This is exactly the level-\(\kappa\)
   170	no-log lemma already derived and consumed in SHEET6-DIRECTIONB.md Section 7.
   171	For residue A, \(\kappa=42\): nine placewise coefficients vanish, of which the
   172	six live emitted variables are
   173	
   174	\[
   175	 tf1_{42},tf2_{42},tg1_{42},tg2_{42},tg01_{42},tg02_{42}.
   176	\]
   177	
   178	So (1.7) is a valid necessary condition, but it is not new to the current
   179	repository state and it did not empty the residue-A window.
   180	
   181	---
   182	
   183	## 2. Positive controls: elementary and tame automorphisms
   184	
   185	Normalize \(c=1\).
   186	
   187	### 2.1 One elementary automorphism
   188	
   189	For
   190	
   191	\[
   192	 (P,Q)=(x,y+x^n),
   193	\]
   194	
   195	direct calculation gives
   196	
   197	\[
   198	 \begin{aligned}
   199	 P\,dQ-x\,dy&=n x^n\,dx
   200	   =d\!\left(\frac n{n+1}x^{n+1}\right),\\
   201	 Q\,dP-y\,dx&=x^n\,dx
   202	   =d\!\left(\frac1{n+1}x^{n+1}\right).
   203	 \end{aligned}                                             \tag{2.1}
   204	\]
   205	
   206	In the \(X\ne0\) infinity chart, the first primitive is
   207	\(\frac n{n+1}u^{-(n+1)}\), and its action form is
   208	\(-n u^{-(n+2)}du\). It has a high-order pole but zero residue. The exact
   209	SymPy control used \(n=4\): primitive pole order \(5\), action-form normal
   210	pole order \(6\), residue \(0\).
   211	
   212	### 2.2 A tame product with elementary degrees \(3,4\)
   213	
   214	Let
   215	
   216	\[
   217	 V_3(x,y)=(x,y+x^3),\qquad H_4(x,y)=(x+y^4,y),
   218	\]
   219	
   220	and put \(F=H_4\circ V_3\). With \(R=y+x^3\),
   221	
   222	\[
   223	 P=x+R^4,\qquad Q=R,\qquad [P,Q]=1.                         \tag{2.2}
   224	\]
   225	
   226	Both primitives are explicit:
   227	
   228	\[
   229	 S_P=\frac34x^4+\frac15R^5,
   230	 \qquad
   231	 S_Q=\frac14x^4+\frac45R^5.                               \tag{2.3}
   232	\]
   233	
   234	Indeed \(dS_P=P\,dQ-x\,dy\), \(dS_Q=Q\,dP-y\,dx\), and
   235	\(S_P+S_Q=PQ-xy=x^4+R^5\). At infinity,
   236	
   237	\[
   238	 S_P=\frac{3}{4u^4}+\frac{(1+u^2v)^5}{5u^{15}},
   239	 \qquad \lim_{u\to0}u^{15}S_P=\frac15.                   \tag{2.4}
   240	\]
   241	
   242	Thus the primitive has generic pole order \(15\), \(dS_P\) has normal pole
   243	order \(16\), and its divisorial residue is still \(0\). Large action poles are
   244	therefore legal even for tame maps; pole order alone cannot distinguish tame
   245	from counterexample data.
   246	
   247	---
   248	
   249	## 3. Residue-A leading pair without tails
   250	
   251	Use exactly the merge chart of SHEET6-DIRECTIONB.md Section 0, with the
   252	registered gauge \(\sigma=6\):
   253	
   254	\[
   255	 x=t^{-42},\qquad y=t^{12}+\eta t^{32},
   256	\]
   257	
   258	where setting the registered \(t^{18},t^{24},t^{30}\) prefix freedoms and all
   259	later tails to zero is the requested zero-tail test. Set
   260	
   261	\[
   262	 r(\eta)=(\eta^3-(3+\sqrt3))(\eta^3-(3-\sqrt3))
   263	         =\eta^6-6\eta^3+6,
   264	\]
   265	
   266	\[
   267	 S_M=\frac{7^{12}}{2^6},\qquad G_M=-\frac{7^{18}}{2^9},
   268	 \qquad C=S_MG_M,
   269	\]
   270	
   271	and take the leading pair
   272	
   273	\[
   274	 f_0=S_Mt^{-12}r^2,\qquad g_0=G_Mt^{-18}r^3.               \tag{3.1}
   275	\]
   276	
   277	The exact wedge calculation is
   278	
   279	\[
   280	 dx\wedge dy=-42t^{-11}dt\wedge d\eta,
   281	 \qquad df_0\wedge dg_0=0.                                \tag{3.2}
   282	\]
   283	
   284	The second equality is the familiar \(2{:}3\) leading cancellation: both
   285	coordinates are powers of the same \(r\), with matching \(t\)-weights. Moreover
   286	
   287	\[
   288	 f_0\,dg_0=d\!\left(\frac35Ct^{-30}r^5\right).             \tag{3.3}
   289	\]
   290	
   291	For the truncated action
   292	\(\alpha_0=f_0\,dg_0-x\,dy=A_tdt+A_\eta d\eta\),
   293	
   294	\[
   295	 \begin{aligned}
   296	 A_t&=-18Cr^5t^{-31}-12t^{-31}-32\eta t^{-11},\\
   297	 A_\eta&=3Cr^4r't^{-30}-t^{-10},\\
   298	 d\alpha_0&=42t^{-11}dt\wedge d\eta.                     \tag{3.4}
   299	 \end{aligned}
   300	\]
   301	
   302	This has three consequences.
   303	
   304	1. The zero-tail leading pair has no action primitive, because it is not itself
   305	   Keller. This is not a new contradiction: \(42t^{-11}\) is exactly the
   306	   inhomogeneous slot-20 Jacobian defect. SHEET6-DIRECTIONB.md already proves
   307	   that nonzero tails must cancel it and that the zero-extension locus is dead.
   308	2. Nevertheless its divisorial \(dt/t\) coefficient is \(0\), and on every
   309	   constant-\(\eta\) root of \(r\) the fibre-action residues
   310	   \(\operatorname{Res}(-y\,dx)\) and \(\operatorname{Res}(-x\,dy)\) are both
   311	   \(0\). Thus the proposed leading polar-residue test does **not** kill the
   312	   residue-A lead.
   313	3. Adding a level-42 coefficient \(c_{42}t^{42}\) to \(y\) gives exactly
   314	
   315	   \[
   316	   \operatorname{Res}_t(y\,dx)=-42c_{42},\qquad
   317	   \operatorname{Res}_t(x\,dy)=42c_{42},                  \tag{3.5}
   318	   \]
   319	
   320	   reproducing PIN42 and nothing stronger.
   321	
   322	The primitive polar divisor is therefore not “illegal for the \((2,3)\)
   323	genome.” Before completion there is no primitive because Row 20 is missing;
   324	after any Keller completion, a polynomial primitive exists automatically and
   325	all of its canonical residues vanish automatically.
   326	
