   386	
   387	## 5. Relation to the vertex-gap residue
   388	
   389	The action residue (1.6) is **not** the paper's nontrivial
   390	\(R_{k,d_2}\). The repository has already isolated the distinction in
   391	RESIDUE.md and cases/residue_check.py.
   392	
   393	For the strip equation \([P,Q]=x^k\), the analogous action form
   394	
   395	\[
   396	 P\,dQ-\frac{x^{k+1}}{k+1}\,dy                            \tag{5.1}
   397	\]
   398	
   399	is again closed and polynomial-exact. On the binomial strip locus
   400	\(A=1+a_2y\), however, solving the *support-restricted column ODE* produces
   401	
   402	\[
   403	 \operatorname{Res}_{y=-1/a_2}
   404	 \frac{2BC'-kB'C}{A^{k+2}}\,dy
   405	 =(k+2)(-1)^k a_2^{-2d_2-1}R_{k,d_2}.                    \tag{5.2}
   406	\]
   407	
   408	That is a residue of a boundary-twisted reconstruction integrand. Equivalently,
   409	it is an iterated residue of \(dP\wedge dQ\) only after inserting the toric
   410	boundary factors \(x^{k+2}A^{k+2}\). The twist contains exactly the strip,
   411	gap, and chosen-boundary information. Without it, the natural candidate is
   412	exact:
   413	
   414	\[
   415	 \frac{dP\wedge dQ}{P^{k+2}}
   416	 =d\!\left(-\frac{P^{-(k+1)}}{k+1}\,dQ\right),            \tag{5.3}
   417	\]
   418	
   419	so its total iterated residue is identically zero.
   420	
   421	At the vertex-gap cell \((k,d_2)=(2,2)\), write
   422	
   423	\[
   424	 A=1+ay,\quad C=y+\frac a2y^2,\quad
   425	 B=b_2y^2+b_3y^3+b_4y^4.
   426	\]
   427	
   428	SymPy gives
   429	
   430	\[
   431	 \operatorname{Res}_{y=-1/a}
   432	 \frac{2BC'-2B'C}{A^4}\,dy=\frac{4b_4}{a^5},             \tag{5.4}
   433	\]
   434	
   435	and \(R_{2,2}=b_4\). The repository's exact Fraction engine was also rerun:
   436	python3 cases/residue_check.py ended ALL OK on its 11 cells, including
   437	the exactness control (5.3).
   438	
   439	Hence the comparison is precise:
   440	
   441	- The global action residue is identically zero for every Keller pair.
   442	- The placewise fibre residue is the already-banked level-\(\kappa\) no-log pin.
   443	- In the strip block, the nonzero boundary-twisted reconstruction residue is
   444	  exactly the existing vertex-gap functional \(R_{k,d_2}\).
   445	- Residue A is not killed by either canonical action residue; its zero-tail
   446	  failure is exactly the already-known slot-20 Jacobian defect.
   447	
   448	The two residue packages share a “no logarithm when integrating a Jacobian
   449	equation” philosophy, but they are not independent constraints. Recovering
   450	\(R_{k,d_2}\) requires reintroducing the Newton/toric boundary data that the
   451	putatively global action route was meant to transcend.
   452	
   453	---
   454	
   455	## 6. Does this attack the landing gap G2?
   456	
   457	No.
   458	
   459	The action primitive is universal in the wrong sense: it exists for every
   460	Keller pair before any infinity tree or book is chosen. Its canonical residues
   461	therefore cannot select a finite entry, bound topological degree, type all
   462	downstream branches, or prove that an arbitrary counterexample lands in an
   463	enumerated configuration. The tame and \(\Lambda=3\) controls show that even the
   464	primitive's pole order and complete residue vector do not encode sheet number.
   465	
   466	A mixed-coordinate generating function does not repair this. The polynomial
   467	\(S_P(x,y)\) from (1.3) is not automatically a polynomial function of
   468	\((x,Q)\). Passing to \(S(x,Q)\) requires the projection
   469	\((x,y)\mapsto(x,Q)\) to be globally invertible, which is already a substantial
   470	coordinate/injectivity assertion.
   471	
   472	> **CONJECTURE (only possible surviving refinement; not established).** On a
   473	> completely specified resolved boundary, the *full principal parts* of all
   474	> local action primitives might obey a nontrivial Cousin/Mittag-Leffler gluing
   475	> condition not implied by the filed local Jacobian rows.
   476	
   477	The experiment supplies no positive evidence for this conjecture. A version
   478	using only pole orders or residues is refuted by Sections 2 and 4. A version
   479	using full principal parts requires all boundary components, intersection
   480	charts, and tail coefficients—the compactification/algebraization problem
   481	again—and still would not by itself supply a typed terminating map into the
   482	current books. It should not be advertised as a G2 repair.
   483	
   484	### Final classification
   485	
   486	| Candidate output of row 33 | Result |
   487	|---|---|
   488	| Polynomial global action primitive | **EXACT**, but automatic for every Keller pair |
   489	| Divisorial action residues on any resolution | **EXACTLY ZERO**, hence hollow |
   490	| Action periods | **EXACTLY ZERO**, hence hollow |
   491	| Per-fibre residues \(\operatorname{Res}(y\,dx),\operatorname{Res}(x\,dy)\) | **EXACT necessary condition**, already PIN42; no residue-A kill |
   492	| Polar order of the primitive | Legal and arbitrarily large on tame maps |
   493	| Residue-A zero-tail failure | Exactly Row 20 / \(J=1\), not a new obstruction |
   494	| Boundary-twisted strip residue | Exactly \(R_{k,d_2}\), the vertex-gap theorem |
   495	| Three-sheet discrimination | Fails on the exact local \(\Lambda=3\) control |
   496	| Universal book landing / G2 | No progress |
   497	
   498	**Operational verdict:** retain the level-\(\kappa\) no-log equations as cheap,
   499	depth-independent necessary rows. Do not spend a new campaign on untwisted
   500	global action residues or primitive pole orders. Any future residue work should
   501	be honestly filed as a boundary-twisted Newton/adjoint extension of
   502	\(R_{k,d_2}\), not as a new global symplectic lane.
   503	
