    30	## 1. What a Jung descent step would have to be
    31	
    32	Let \(F=(P,Q)\in\mathbf C[x,y]^2\), with
    33	
    34	\[
    35	 J(P,Q)=1,\qquad p=\deg P,\quad q=\deg Q,\quad
    36	 \Delta(F)=\max(p,q).
    37	\]
    38	
    39	The relevant elementary target automorphisms are
    40	
    41	\[
    42	 E_\phi^{(2)}(U,V)=(U,V-\phi(U)),\qquad
    43	 E_\phi^{(1)}(U,V)=(U-\phi(V),V).
    44	\]
    45	
    46	Postcomposition gives
    47	
    48	\[
    49	 E_\phi^{(2)}\circ F=(P,Q-\phi(P)),\qquad
    50	 E_\phi^{(1)}\circ F=(P-\phi(Q),Q).
    51	\]
    52	
    53	It preserves the constant Jacobian. It also preserves noninvertibility: if
    54	\(E_\phi\circ F\) were an automorphism, then
    55	\(F=E_\phi^{-1}\circ(E_\phi\circ F)\) would be one. Therefore, if every
    56	nonlinear Keller pair admitted such a strict drop, a counterexample of
    57	minimal \(\Delta\) could not exist.
    58	
    59	This is the exact point at which Jung--van der Kulk cannot simply be quoted.
    60	The amalgam theorem decomposes an element already known to lie in
    61	\(\operatorname{Aut}(\mathbf A^2)\). A counterexample is an endomorphism
    62	outside that group; the lowering factor must be produced from additional
    63	information.
    64	
    65	### Lemma 1 (exact elementary drop criterion)
    66	
    67	Assume \(p<q\), and write \(P_p,Q_q\) for the ordinary top homogeneous
    68	forms. Let
    69	
    70	\[
    71	 \phi(T)=a_kT^k+\cdots,\qquad a_k\ne0.
    72	\]
    73	
    74	Then
    75	
    76	\[
    77	 \Delta(P,Q-\phi(P))<q
    78	\]
    79	
    80	if and only if
    81	
    82	\[
    83	 q=kp,\qquad Q_q=a_kP_p^k.
    84	 \tag{1.1}
    85	\]
    86	
    87	Indeed, \(P\) remains of degree \(p<q\). The degree-\(q\) part of the second
    88	component can disappear only if \(kp=q\), and in that case it disappears
    89	exactly under the second identity in (1.1). Conversely those identities
    90	make \(\deg(Q-\phi(P))<q\), so the maximum drops. Lower coefficients of
    91	\(\phi\) do not affect this first drop. The case \(q<p\) is symmetric.
    92	
    93	In particular, for unequal component degrees a strict one-shear descent
    94	requires divisibility of the larger degree by the smaller. For \(p=q\), an
    95	invertible affine target change may first lower one linear combination when
    96	\(P_p,Q_p\) are proportional, but an invertible linear change cannot lower
    97	both top forms at once. After that affine preparation the same criterion
    98	(1.1) applies to the resulting unequal degree pair.
    99	
   100	This is exactly the automorphism proof's degree-divisibility step. It is not
   101	forced for a normalized hypothetical counterexample.
