   212	
   213	### Theorem 2.1 — canonical packet flag and proximity path (**PROVED**)
   214	
   215	Let a certified paired packet retain the data listed in §1.  Then it
   216	determines canonically:
   217	
   218	1. a proper or infinitely-near point \(p_\omega\) over the original
   219	   \(\mathbf P^2\);
   220	2. the finite sequence of ordinary point blowups from \(\mathbf P^2\) to
   221	   \(p_\omega\); and
   222	3. the immediate proximity parent or parents of \(p_\omega\).
   223	
   224	The result is independent of a regular fan, a graph resolution of the cut
   225	word, a Kummer presentation, a root coordinate, and an allowed change of
   226	pencil generator.  Two packet presentations give the same infinitely-near
   227	point if and only if their finite point sequences agree.  Equivalently, their
   228	terminal centers agree on a common carrier model on which the relevant
   229	predecessor divisors have been realized and the terminal point has not yet
   230	been blown up.
   231	
   232	#### Proof
   233	
   234	An integral Laurent cut is an automorphism of the function field.  Compose
   235	the valuation (1.1) with the inverse of the exact cut prefix.  This gives a
   236	rank-two valuation of the original field \(K\).  In the fractional case use
   237	the restriction constructed in §1.2.  Thus in all cases the packet produces
   238	one intrinsic valuation of \(K\), not merely a coordinate root token.
   239	
   240	Every divisorial valuation of the function field of a smooth surface is
   241	obtained by a finite sequence of point blowups.  Concretely here, insert the
   242	primitive weight ray by the Euclidean regular subdivision; every inserted ray
   243	is a point blowup.  The first component of (1.1) is realized by a prime
   244	divisor \(E\), and the second component is the closed point on \(E\) selected
   245	by the residue root.  If the cut prefix has indeterminacies, resolve its graph
   246	and then take the center of the pulled-back valuation.  This construction uses
   247	the projection to the original plane automatically, because the valuation is
   248	already a valuation of its function field.
   249	
   250	The valuative criterion of properness gives a unique center on every proper
   251	model.  Given two fan or graph realizations of the same packet, pass to a
   252	common regular carrier on which its divisorial component is present but its
   253	selected residue point has not yet been blown up.  Their displayed roots have
   254	the same center there because they define the same flag valuation.  The
   255	minimal sequence of centers needed to reach that divisor, followed by the
   256	residue point, is therefore independent of the realization.  Conversely,
   257	different terminal centers on such a carrier give different point IDs.
   258	
   259	At the final point, one or two exceptional components pass through the
   260	center.  Their creators are, by definition, the immediate proximity parent or
   261	parents.  They are read from the canonical point-blowup sequence.  A nonzero
   262	toric residue root is away from the toric fixed points and is therefore a
   263	free point on that toric realization; a later graph or quotient presentation
   264	may display the same point differently, but the common-model definition also
   265	covers satellite points.  Pencil-generator changes do not change either
   266	two-generated base ideal and hence do not change the valuation center.
   267	\(\square\)
