   421	
   422	Restricting \(H_c\) to \(E\) kills \(B\) and leaves \(u(r)r^N\), proving
   423	(4.2).  For one blowup at a point of two curves with no common component,
   424	Noether's formula is
   425	
   426	\[
   427	 I_p(H_c,E)=m_p(H_c)m_p(E)
   428	       +\sum_{q\mapsto p}I_q(H_c',E').                \tag{4.4}
   429	\]
   430	
   431	Here \(E\) is smooth, so \(m_p(E)=1\), and only the unique point where its
   432	strict transform meets the new exceptional divisor can contribute to the
   433	remaining intersection with \(E'\).  Iterating (4.4) until separation gives
   434	(4.3).
   435	
   436	At a point where both transformed generators vanish, a general combination
   437	has the generic ideal order, so \(m_i\) is the mobile point-basis coefficient.
   438	If the transformed denominator is a unit, the pencil is already generated by
   439	a unit there and that point is not a base point; any remaining contact belongs
   440	only to the selected numerator member.  Thus the exact denominator transforms
   441	select the base prefix. \(\square\)
   442	
   443	### Corollary 4.2 — proved vector-valued WTC, with exact capacity data
   444	
   445	For the two packet components, the canonical flag and the four actual weak
   446	generators determine two contact chains and their ordinary multiplicity
   447	vectors.  When the denominators survive the full chains,
   448	
   449	\[
   450	 \sum_iR_{p_i}=\alpha T,qquad
   451	 \sum_jS_{q_j}=\beta T.                               \tag{4.5}
   452	\]
   453	
   454	This is the precise proved form of the proposed WTC-VEC for one occurrence.
   455	It includes point names and proximity paths.  It does **not** say that the two
   456	chains coincide, that their entries are pointwise in ratio \(\alpha:\beta\),
   457	or that either vector has one nonzero entry.  Those are exactly the kinds of
   458	extra rigidity needed for PCC's quadratic mass.
   459	
   460	### Corollary 4.3 — KPC is maximal contact concentration
   461	
   462	Under KPC, \(m_0=N\) in (4.3), so every later term is zero.  Conversely, if
   463	the full contact number is carried by the first mobile base coefficient, then
   464	the corresponding KPC containment holds.  Thus WTC-1 does not merely
   465	transport weight; it asserts the extremal, no-splitting case of Noether's
   466	contact identity.
   467	
   468	---
   469	
   470	## 5. Exact separation example
   471	
   472	This section strengthens the diagnostic examples in the first round.  It
   473	keeps a constant Laurent Jacobian, the paired same-core leading powers, a
   474	nonzero root, and both denominator capacities simultaneously.
   475	
   476	### Proposition 5.1 — paired Laurent Jacobian packet data do not force KPC
   477	(**PROVED**)
   478	
   479	In \(k[u^{\pm1},v]\), set
   480	
   481	\[
   482	 R=u^3v-u^{-1},\qquad
   483	 P=R^2+u^{-1},\qquad
   484	 Q=R^3+\frac32u^{-1}R.                                \tag{5.1}
   485	\]
   486	
   487	Then
   488	
   489	\[
   490	 [P,Q]=-\frac32.                                      \tag{5.2}
   491	\]
   492	
   493	For \(w=(-1,4)\),
   494	
   495	\[
   496	 v_w(P)=2,\quad v_w(Q)=3,\qquad
   497	 \ell_w(P)=R^2,\quad\ell_w(Q)=R^3.                   \tag{5.3}
   498	\]
   499	
   500	Also
   501	
   502	\[
   503	 \frac{v_{1,1}(P)}{v_{1,1}(Q)}=\frac8{12}=\frac23,
   504	 \qquad
   505	 \frac{v_{0,1}(P)}{v_{0,1}(Q)}=\frac23.              \tag{5.4}
   506	\]
   507	
   508	On the regular root chart
   509	
   510	\[
   511	 x=u,qquad r=u^4v-1,                                 \tag{5.5}
   512	\]
   513	
   514	the marked factor of \(R=u^{-1}r\) is the nonzero residue root
   515	\(u^4v=1\).  The rational functions and their weak pencil ideals are
   516	
   517	\[
   518	 \begin{aligned}
   519	 P&=\frac{r^2+x}{x^2},& I_f&=(r^2+x,x^2),\\
   520	 Q&=\frac{r^3+\frac32xr}{x^3},&
   521	 I_g&=(r^3+\tfrac32xr,x^3).                           \tag{5.6}
   522	 \end{aligned}
   523	\]
   524	
   525	Their boundary restrictions are \(r^2,r^3\), and the denominator orders are
   526	exactly \(2,3\), but
   527	
   528	\[
   529	 \operatorname{ord}I_f=1,qquad
   530	 \operatorname{ord}I_g=2.                             \tag{5.7}
   531	\]
   532	
   533	After blowing up in the common tangent direction, their \(E\)-contact
   534	base-prefix vectors are respectively
   535	
   536	\[
   537	 (1,1),\qquad(2,1),                                   \tag{5.8}
   538	\]
   539	
   540	whose sums are \(2,3\).
   541	
   542	#### Proof
   543	
   544	Expanding (5.1) gives
   545	
   546	\[
   547	\begin{aligned}
   548	P={}&u^6v^2-2u^2v+u^{-2}+u^{-1},\\
   549	Q={}&u^9v^3-3u^5v^2+3uv-u^{-3}
   550	     +\frac32u^2v-\frac32u^{-2}.
   551	\end{aligned}                                         \tag{5.9}
   552	\]
   553	
   554	Direct differentiation gives (5.2).  Every term of \(R\) has \(w\)-degree
   555	one.  The extra term of \(P\) has degree one rather than two, and the extra
   556	term of \(Q\) has degree two rather than three; this proves (5.3).  The
   557	maximum ordinary and \(v\)-degrees in (5.9) give (5.4).  Substitution (5.5)
   558	gives (5.6), from which (5.7) is immediate.
   559	
   560	For the first ideal, use the blowup chart \(x=rx_1\):
   561	
   562	\[
   563	 (r^2+x,x^2)=r\,(r+x_1,rx_1^2),                      \tag{5.10}
   564	\]
   565	
   566	so the weak orders on the contact with the strict transform of \(E\) are
   567	\(1\) and then \(1\).  For the second,
   568	
   569	\[
   570	 (r^3+\tfrac32xr,x^3)
   571	 =r^2\,(r+\tfrac32x_1,rx_1^3),                       \tag{5.11}
   572	\]
   573	
   574	so they are \(2\) and then \(1\).  At the second common point the two
   575	numerator tangents are \(r+x_1=0\) and \(r+\frac32x_1=0\); hence any further
   576	base points lie over distinct points of the next exceptional divisor.  This
   577	proves (5.8) and shows that there is no later common base center. \(\square\)
   578	
   579	At the two common base points in this example,
   580	
   581	\[
   582	 \min\!\left(\left\lfloor\frac{R_p}{2}\right\rfloor,
   583	              \left\lfloor\frac{S_p}{3}\right\rfloor\right)=0, \tag{5.12}
   584	\]
   585	
   586	so the predicted packet weight \(T=1\) produces no PCC unit.  Further centers
   587	belong to at most one pencil cluster and also have \(h_p=0\).
   588	
   589	### Scope of the example
   590	
   591	Proposition 5.1 is **not** a counterexample to the plane Jacobian conjecture,
   592	to PCC, or to KPC as stated.  The pair is Laurent rather than polynomial, has
   593	no claimed Sigray/minimal-pair provenance, and its displayed common-power edge
   594	is not claimed to be an eligible *output occurrence* of the full Corollary-7.4
   595	prefix (in particular, the starting-direction/F-data have not been supplied).
   596	
   597	It is decisive for the reduction perimeter: it satisfies the paired power,
   598	both denominator capacities, and even the constant Laurent Jacobian.  Hence
   599	KPC must use a hypothesis absent from that local package—most plausibly the
   600	polynomial origin together with the whole eligible direction/cut prefix.
   601	Repeating the cleared-Jacobian coefficient induction of the first round
   602	without that global input cannot prove KPC.
   603	
   604	### Proposition 5.2 — a general formal monomial-Jacobian separation family
   605	(**PROVED**)
   606	
   607	Let \(2\le\alpha<\beta\), \(T,L,k\in\mathbf N\), \(L\ge T\), and
   608	\(k<\alpha T\).  Put
   609	
   610	\[
   611	 f=\frac{r^{\alpha T}+x^k}{x^{\alpha L}},\qquad
   612	 g=\frac{r^{\beta T}+(\beta/\alpha)x^k
   613	                   r^{(\beta-\alpha)T}}{x^{\beta L}}. \tag{5.13}
   614	\]
   615	
   616	Then the boundary packet and denominator capacities are exactly the predicted
   617	ones, while
   618	
   619	\[
   620	 \operatorname{ord}I_f=k<\alpha T,qquad
   621	 \operatorname{ord}I_g=k+(\beta-\alpha)T<\beta T.     \tag{5.14}
   622	\]
   623	
   624	Nevertheless the pulled-back Jacobian is the single boundary monomial
   625	
   626	\[
   627	 [f,g]=
   628	 \frac{\beta}{\alpha}(\beta-\alpha)T(k-\alpha L)
   629	 x^{2k-(\alpha+\beta)L-1}
   630	 r^{(\beta-\alpha)T-1}.                               \tag{5.15}
   631	\]
   632	
   633	#### Proof
   634	
   635	The order assertions are immediate from \(L\ge T\) and \(k<\alpha T\).
   636	Differentiate (5.13) after writing the two numerators as \(A,G\).  In the
   637	cleared bracket
   638	
   639	\[
   640	 x(A_xG_r-A_rG_x)+\beta L A_rG-\alpha LAG_r,          \tag{5.16}
   641	\]
   642	
   643	the pure \(r^{(\alpha+\beta)T-1}\) terms cancel because the exponent and
   644	denominator ratios are both \(\alpha:\beta\).  The
   645	\(x^kr^{\beta T-1}\) terms cancel because the coefficient in (5.13) is
   646	\(\beta/\alpha\).  The sole remaining term is (5.15). \(\square\)
   647	
   648	The specialization \((\alpha,\beta,T,L,k)=(2,3,1,1,1)\) has no \(r\)-factor
   649	on the right and becomes Proposition 5.1 after the root chart (5.5).
   650	
