   706	## 7. Canonical no-double-counting after KPC
   707	
   708	Let \(\Omega\) be the factor-expanded all-root occurrence forest, and let
   709	
   710	\[
   711	 w(\omega)=q_\omega t_\omega.                         \tag{7.1}
   712	\]
   713	
   714	Theorem 2.1 assigns to every occurrence an intrinsic infinitely-near point
   715	ID.  It is useful to make the equivalence relation explicit.
   716	
   717	### Definition 7.1 — point-ID equivalence
   718	
   719	Write \(\omega\sim\omega'\) if their finite Enriques point sequences
   720	terminate at the same infinitely-near point (and therefore have the same
   721	proximity relations).  Equivalently, on a common carrier model realizing the
   722	required predecessor divisors and not yet blowing up either terminal point,
   723	the two flags have the same closed center.
   724	
   725	Equality of bare root scalars, Laurent factors, or divisorial valuations is
   726	neither necessary nor sufficient.  At a satellite point two divisor-based
   727	presentations can name the same closed center; conversely, the same root
   728	scalar in two unrelated charts can name different points.
   729	
   730	### Proposition 7.2 — identity/no-double-counting relation (**PROVED**)
   731	
   732	The relation in Definition 7.1 is a canonical equivalence relation,
   733	independent of all packet chart choices.  A next-cut occurrence is a new
   734	center exactly when its Enriques sequence properly extends the old sequence;
   735	the extension records its proximity edge or edges.
   736	
   737	#### Proof
   738	
   739	Equality of finite infinitely-near point sequences is an equivalence
   740	relation.  A common carrier model converts that equality into equality of
   741	terminal closed centers.  Blowing up a terminal point replaces it by later
   742	points, so equality is deliberately defined by the finite sequence rather
   743	than by centers on every higher refinement.  Theorem 2.1 proves that changing
   744	a packet presentation does not change its sequence. \(\square\)
   745	
   746	Thus the *bookkeeping* half of PCC-COVER is no longer conjectural at theorem
   747	level.  It still needs an implementation record if the Section-4 engine is to
   748	compute it.
   749	
   750	Assume KPC.  Let \(\kappa:\Omega\to K_f\cap K_g\) be the resulting point map
   751	and define conservatively
   752	
   753	\[
   754	 c_p=\max\bigl(\{w(\omega):\kappa(\omega)=p\}\cup\{0\}\bigr).     \tag{7.2}
   755	\]
   756	
   757	Then
   758	
   759	\[
   760	 c_p\le
   761	 \min\!\left(\left\lfloor\frac{R_p}{\alpha}\right\rfloor,
   762	              \left\lfloor\frac{S_p}{\beta}\right\rfloor\right)
   763	 =h_p.                                                \tag{7.3}
   764	\]
   765	
   766	In the exact-equality form of KPC, two occurrences at the same center must in
   767	fact have the same weight; the maximum convention remains the safe definition
   768	for duplicated certificates and for any future lower-bound version of WTC.
   769	
