   505	
   506	## 5. Gap ledger and decision
   507	
   508	### Exact conclusions
   509	
   510	1. The first post-top quintic equation module is the unique
   511	   \(S_{(13,2,2,2)}V\), of dimension 364.
   512	2. On a genuinely ternary HC4 top cone it forces
   513	   \(\partial_{vv}h_4=0\) in the vertex direction.
   514	3. On the \(y\)-linear sector its complete restriction is \(2a_6a_5\), hence
   515	   zero modulo the radical of the top equation \(a_6^2=0\).
   516	4. The relevant sector isotype has multiplicity two, but the ambient GL4
   517	   layer selects only the factored, already-vanishing copy.
   518	5. The explicit Meng–Yang HC5 counterexample has no constant affine or
   519	   unit-quadratic pivot, so their Schur descent does not iterate 5→4 through
   520	   any linear rechart.
   521	
   522	### CONJECTURE gaps
   523	
   524	- **CONJECTURE:** no later cone-stratified HC4 layer yields a degree-uniform
   525	  proof mechanism on the cotangent sector. The present computation settles
   526	  only the first layer; it supports this statement but does not prove it.
   527	- **CONJECTURE:** no nonlinear rechart or different partial-Legendre device
   528	  descends the Meng–Yang example to four variables. Only the exact
   529	  constant-linear pivot class used by their theorem was killed.
   530	- The full quintic HC4 case, including binary/unary cone strata and all lower
   531	  coupled layers, remains open. No quintic HC4 theorem or counterexample is
   532	  claimed here.
   533	
   534	### Row-10 decision
   535	
   536	The first GL4 module has **bulk HC4 leverage but no JC2-sector leverage**. It
   537	reproduces a useful cone-direction constraint generically, yet that constraint
   538	is automatic on cotangent potentials and its restricted equation is killed by
   539	the preceding plane top equation. The 5→4 counterexample factory also
   540	self-terminates exactly as advertised.
   541	
   542	**KILL-PROBE verdict: NO LEVERAGE. Confirm the “no new theorem” discount.**
   543	Keep HC4 quintic as an intrinsically interesting four-dimensional problem,
   544	but do not promote its first isotypic layer as evidence for a new plane
   545	theorem.
