    64	## 1. The coordinate-cusp theorem
    65	
    66	For positive weights \(w=(r,s)\), write
    67	
    68	\[
    69	 \deg_w\!\left(\sum a_{ij}U^iV^j\right)
    70	   =\max_{a_{ij}\ne0}(ri+sj)
    71	\]
    72	
    73	and let \(\operatorname{in}_w h\) be the part of maximal \(w\)-degree.  A
    74	polynomial \(h\in\mathbf C[U,V]\) is a **coordinate** if it is one component
    75	of a polynomial automorphism.
    76	
    77	### Theorem 1.1 (coordinate-cusp exclusion)
    78	
    79	Let \(r,s>1\) be coprime and let \(c,d\in\mathbf C^*\).  If \(h\) is a
    80	coordinate, then
    81	
    82	\[
    83	 \operatorname{in}_{r,s}h\notin
    84	 \bigl(d^rU^s-c^sV^r\bigr).
    85	 \tag{1.1}
    86	\]
    87	
    88	Equivalently,
    89	
    90	\[
    91	 \operatorname{in}_{r,s}h(cZ^r,dZ^s)\ne0.
    92	 \tag{1.2}
    93	\]
    94	
    95	#### Proof
    96	
    97	Choose a coordinate mate \(k\) with \(J(h,k)=\lambda\in\mathbf C^*\), and
    98	set
    99	
   100	\[
   101	 \partial=J(h,-).
   102	\]
   103	
   104	In the coordinates \((h,k)\), \(\partial=\lambda\,\partial/\partial k\).
   105	Thus \(\partial\) is a locally nilpotent derivation (LND).
   106	
   107	Put \(\bar h=\operatorname{in}_{r,s}h\).  The highest filtered homogeneous
   108	part of \(\partial\) is
   109	
   110	\[
   111	 D=J(\bar h,-).
   112	\]
   113	
   114	This \(D\) is also locally nilpotent.  Here is the filtered argument in the
   115	form needed below.  The derivation \(D\) is homogeneous for the
   116	\((r,s)\)-grading.  If it were not locally nilpotent, some homogeneous
   117	\(q\) would have \(D^j(q)\ne0\) for every \(j\ge0\).  Inductively,
   118	\(D^j(q)\) is then the highest homogeneous part of \(\partial^j(q)\).
   119	Consequently no \(\partial^j(q)\) could vanish, contradicting local
   120	nilpotence of \(\partial\).  Also \(D\ne0\), because \(\bar h\) is
   121	nonconstant in characteristic zero.
   122	
   123	For a nonzero element \(q\), define its LND degree
   124	
   125	\[
   126	 \delta_D(q)=\max\{j:D^j(q)\ne0\}.
   127	\]
   128	
   129	The Leibniz formula in a characteristic-zero domain gives
   130	
   131	\[
   132	 \delta_D(q_1q_2)=\delta_D(q_1)+\delta_D(q_2).
   133	 \tag{1.3}
   134	\]
   135	
   136	Indeed, if the two degrees are \(e_1,e_2\), the only nonzero term of
   137	\(D^{e_1+e_2}(q_1q_2)\) is
   138	\(\binom{e_1+e_2}{e_1}D^{e_1}q_1D^{e_2}q_2\).  In particular
   139	\(\ker D\) is factorially closed: if a product lies in \(\ker D\), both
   140	factors lie in \(\ker D\).
   141	
   142	Assume for contradiction that
   143	
   144	\[
   145	 \bar h=Bq,\qquad B=d^rU^s-c^sV^r.
   146	\]
   147	
   148	Since \(D(\bar h)=J(\bar h,\bar h)=0\), factorial closure gives
   149	\(B\in\ker D\).  Hence
   150	
   151	\[
   152	 s d^rU^{s-1}D(U)=r c^sV^{r-1}D(V).
   153	 \tag{1.4}
   154	\]
   155	
   156	Coprimality of the powers of \(U\) and \(V\) in the polynomial ring implies
   157	
   158	\[
   159	 V^{r-1}\mid D(U),\qquad U^{s-1}\mid D(V).
   160	 \tag{1.5}
   161	\]
   162	
   163	Neither \(D(U)\) nor \(D(V)\) is zero: by (1.4), one vanishes if and only if
   164	the other does, which would give \(D=0\).  Set
   165	\(p=\delta_D(U)\) and \(q_0=\delta_D(V)\).  Since
   166	\(\delta_D(Da)=\delta_D(a)-1\) whenever \(D(a)\ne0\), (1.5) and (1.3) give
   167	
   168	\[
   169	 p-1\ge(r-1)q_0,\qquad q_0-1\ge(s-1)p.
   170	\]
   171	
   172	Adding and rearranging yields
   173	
   174	\[
   175	 (s-2)p+(r-2)q_0\le-2,
   176	\]
   177	
   178	which is impossible because \(r,s\ge2\) and \(p,q_0\ge0\).  This proves
   179	(1.1).
   180	

